  "Number": 19,
  "Question": "If \\(2x-3y-z=0\\) and \\(x+3y-14z=0,\\) \\(z\\ne0,\\) the numerical value of \\(\\dfrac{x^2+3xy}{y^2+z^2}\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(7\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(0\\)",
    "D": "\\(-\\dfrac{20}{17}\\)",
    "E": "\\(-2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The \\(y\\)-terms have opposite coefficients, so adding the equations removes \\(y\\):\n\\[\n3x-15z=0,\n\\qquad x=5z.\n\\]\nSubstituting into \\(x+3y-14z=0\\) gives \\(3y=9z\\), so \\(y=3z\\). These relations express every term in the requested fraction using the same variable:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2+3xy}{y^2+z^2}\n=\\frac{(5z)^2+3(5z)(3z)}{(3z)^2+z^2}\n=\\frac{70z^2}{10z^2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(z\\ne0\\), the denominator \\(10z^2\\) is nonzero and cancellation is valid. The value is \\(\\boxed{7}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
